@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Medium DMN Decision Requirements Diagram for product pricing:
'                 a chain of three decisions (eligibility, rate, quote) sharing
'                 one Business Knowledge Model and several input data sources.
'   usecase:      Teaching decision-to-decision chaining, a reused Business
'                 Knowledge Model fanning out to two decisions, and mixing
'                 information requirements (solid) with knowledge requirements
'                 (dashed). 6-8 DRG elements.
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       retail / product pricing
' ============================================================================

title DMN Decision Requirements - Medium Pricing DRD

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' This DRD shows a DECISION CHAIN: the output of one decision becomes input to
' the next (eligibility -> rate -> quote). Each solid arrow between decisions is
' still an "Information Requirement" — downstream decisions CONSUME upstream
' decision outputs exactly like they consume raw Input Data.
'
' New concept here vs the simple example:
'   - A single Business Knowledge Model can be REUSED by multiple decisions.
'     "Rate Card" is invoked by both "Risk Tier" and "Final Rate". This is the
'     DMN way to express shared, owned logic — draw the BK once, fan out dashed.
'   - Decisions can depend on decisions (transitive), forming a DAG. No cycles.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' === Input Data (stadium) ===
usecase "Customer\nProfile"   as CustomerProfile
usecase "Product\nCatalog"    as ProductCatalog
usecase "Market\nSignals"     as MarketSignals

' === Business Knowledge Model (clipped rectangle — approximated as rectangle) ===
' Reusable, business-owned logic. Drawn ONCE even though two decisions use it.
rectangle "Rate Card\nBK Model" as RateCard <<businessKnowledge>>

' === Knowledge Source (document) ===
file "Pricing\nStrategy Doc" as StrategyDoc

' === Decisions (rectangles) — the chain ===
rectangle "Eligibility"   as Eligibility   <<decision>>
rectangle "Risk Tier"     as RiskTier      <<decision>>
rectangle "Final Rate"    as FinalRate     <<decision>>
rectangle "Quote"         as Quote         <<decision>>

' === Information requirements (solid) ===
' Raw inputs feed the first decisions.
CustomerProfile --> Eligibility
CustomerProfile --> RiskTier
ProductCatalog  --> Quote
MarketSignals   --> RiskTier
MarketSignals   --> FinalRate

' Decision outputs feed downstream decisions — STILL solid (information req).
Eligibility --> RiskTier
RiskTier    --> FinalRate
FinalRate   --> Quote

' === Knowledge requirements (dashed) ===
' Rate Card BK is invoked by two decisions — drawn once, two dashed edges.
RiskTier  ..> RateCard   : <<authority>>
FinalRate ..> RateCard   : <<authority>>

' Strategy doc governs the overall pricing decision logic.
Quote     ..> StrategyDoc : <<authority>>

note bottom of RateCard
  **Why BK Model, not Input Data?**
  A Business Knowledge Model is logic a business owns and may change
  independently (a rate table, a formula). Input Data is a fact observed
  from outside. Reusing one BK across decisions keeps a single source of
  truth for that logic.
end note

note right of FinalRate
  **Decision chain note**
  Information requirements can connect:
    Input Data   -> Decision
    Decision     -> Decision   (output of one feeds another)
  Both use a SOLID arrow. Only knowledge requirements are dashed.
end note
@enduml
